1. Motherboard Form Factors
  
  - ATX: 12×9.6 in, up to 7 PCIe slots, multiple RAM slots — the most popular/versatile, ideal for gaming PCs, workstations,
    and high-performance builds.
  - MicroATX: 9.6×9.6 in, up to 4 PCIe slots — a balanced, more affordable option for general-purpose PCs and budget/mid-range gaming.
  - Mini-ITX: 6.7×6.7 in, just 1 PCIe slot, limited RAM slots — best for compact/portable builds like small-form-factor PCs and home
    theater systems.
  - Extended ATX (EATX): 12×13 in, larger than ATX — more RAM/PCIe slots and cooling room, used in high-end workstations and enthusiast
    gaming rigs.
  - Mini-STX: 5.8×5.8 in — ultra-small form factor supporting modern CPUs with a few expansion slots, good for budget-friendly, space-saving
    builds.
  - Nano-ITX (4.7×4.7 in) and Pico-ITX (3.9×2.8 in): even smaller, used in embedded systems and IoT devices where space is extremely
    limited, with very limited expansion.
    
    Choosing a form factor: confirm case compatibility, consider expansion needs (PCIe/RAM slots), and factor in cooling/power
    delivery — larger boards generally offer better thermals and more robust power delivery. Trends: more integrated components
    (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, M.2 built in), modular/swappable designs for custom builds, and improved power efficiency in compact form factors.

2. Motherboard Connector Types
  
  Power connectors:
    
    - 24-pin ATX connector — main power supply to the motherboard (CPU, RAM, etc.).
    - 4-pin/8-pin CPU power connector — dedicated CPU power; 8-pin typically for higher-performance processors.
    - SATA power connector — 15-pin, delivers 3.3V/5V/12V to storage devices.
  
  Storage & expansion connectors:
    
    - SATA ports — most common storage interface, up to 6 Gbps (SATA III).
    - M.2/NVMe slots — support much faster NVMe SSDs over PCIe; compact and can also host wireless cards.
    - PCIe slots — used for GPUs, network cards, sound cards, and other expansion cards; speed varies by PCIe version and lane count.
  
  Peripheral & audio connectors:
    
    - USB headers — for internal front-panel USB ports or devices, in USB 2.0/3.0/3.1 variants.
    - HD audio headers — connect front-panel audio (speakers, headphones, mics).
    - RGB/ARGB headers — connect customizable lighting strips/fans.
    
  Network & external I/O:
    
    - Ethernet ports — typically Gigabit (up to 1 Gbps), some boards support 10 Gbps.
    - Rear I/O — USB, audio jacks, HDMI, DisplayPort, etc.; front I/O on the case gives quick access to USB/audio/card readers.
    - Thunderbolt — up to 40 Gbps, can drive external GPUs, storage, and displays.
    - USB-C — reversible, supports fast data transfer, charging, and video output in one connector.
  
3. Motherboard–CPU Compatibility
  
  Three things must align: socket type, chipset support, and BIOS version.
  
  - Socket type must physically and electrically match the CPU — Intel and AMD use different sockets, and mismatches prevent
    installation entirely.
  - Chipset governs communication between CPU and other components, and determines available features (PCIe lanes, overclocking
    support, memory types) even when the socket matches.
  - BIOS updates are sometimes required to support newer CPUs on an existing motherboard, even with a compatible socket and
    chipset — without the right BIOS version, the system may fail to recognize the CPU or boot at all.
  
  Intel socket examples: LGA 1200 (10th/11th gen), LGA 1700 (12th/13th gen, DDR5/PCIe 5.0), LGA 2066 (Core X-series, enthusiast/professional).
  AMD socket examples: AM4 (Ryzen 1000–5000 series), AM5 (latest Ryzen, DDR5), TR4/sTRX4 (Threadripper, workstation-class).
  
  Intel chipset examples: B660 (budget, fewer features), Z690 (high-end, overclocking, PCIe 5.0/DDR5), H770 (balanced). AMD chipset 
  examples: B550/B650 (mainstream, PCIe 4.0/5.0), X570 (premium, full PCIe 4.0, advanced overclocking).
  
  BIOS update best practices: download firmware only from the motherboard manufacturer's site for your exact model, use vendor 
  tools when available (e.g., ASUS EZ Flash, MSI M-Flash), and ensure a stable, uninterrupted power supply during the flash 
  (a UPS is a good idea) — a failed or interrupted update can render the motherboard unusable.

4. BIOS and UEFI
  
  - BIOS (Basic Input/Output System): firmware that initializes hardware at startup and hands off control to the OS; uses a traditional
    text-based interface.
  - UEFI (Unified Extensible Firmware Interface): modern replacement for BIOS — graphical interface (mouse + keyboard), supports drives
    over 2TB, faster boot times, network boot capability, and security features like Secure Boot.
  
  Boot options: boot sequence determines device check order (HDD, USB, optical, network); Secure Boot only allows trusted, signed software
  to load; Fast Boot speeds up startup by skipping certain checks (but can make it harder to enter BIOS/UEFI when needed).
  
  Common settings: power management (sleep states, Wake-on-LAN), overclocking (CPU/RAM/GPU frequency adjustments — requires careful
  thermal monitoring), and hardware monitoring (temperatures, fan speeds, voltages).
  
  Update methods: Windows-based vendor tools, a bootable USB drive with firmware files, or a built-in UEFI flashing utility. Common
  issues & fixes: system won't boot after update → reset or reflash BIOS; misconfigured settings causing instability → review
  overclocking/power/memory settings; hardware not detected → update BIOS or check physical connections. General troubleshooting:
  clear CMOS (jumper or remove the battery) to reset to defaults, roll back to a previous firmware version if an update caused problems,
  and verify boot order.

5. BIOS Security
  
  Key protective features:
  
  - BIOS password protection — restricts who can view/change firmware settings.
  - Firmware integrity checks — compare current firmware against a trusted baseline; alert or block boot if tampering is detected.
  - Secure Boot — only allows signed, trusted bootloaders to execute, blocking rootkits/malware at the boot stage.
  - Boot Guard — protects against unauthorized firmware modification, only allowing signed/trusted firmware to load.
  - Rollback prevention — blocks downgrading to older, vulnerable BIOS versions after an update.
  
  TPM (Trusted Platform Module): a dedicated hardware security chip that securely stores cryptographic keys, passwords, and certificates.
  Used for disk encryption (e.g., with BitLocker), secure authentication, and platform attestation (proving hardware/software configuration
  hasn't been altered).
  
  Configuring BIOS security: enable Secure Boot, set a BIOS password, and keep BIOS/UEFI firmware updated for the latest protections.
  Common troubleshooting: Secure Boot errors → check that the bootloader is properly signed/trusted; TPM not detected → verify it's
  enabled in BIOS/UEFI and firmware is current; locked-out BIOS password → reset via the motherboard's jumper or by removing the CMOS battery.

6. Motherboard Encryption
  
  - TPM (Trusted Platform Module): hardware-based key storage and cryptographic operations; supports disk encryption (BitLocker),
    Secure Boot, Measured Boot (tracks integrity at each boot stage), and remote attestation.
  - HSM (Hardware Security Module): a dedicated, tamper-resistant device that physically isolates cryptographic processing from the
    main system — offers high-performance cryptography and secure key lifecycle management. Common in banking, healthcare, and government
    environments where software-based encryption alone isn't considered secure enough.
  
  Setting up encryption: enable TPM 2.0 in BIOS/UEFI, optionally add/configure an HSM if supported, enable BitLocker (or equivalent) in
  the OS, keep TPM/HSM firmware updated, use strong keys/passwords, and maintain regular backups of encrypted data.
  
  Troubleshooting: TPM not detected → check it's enabled in BIOS/UEFI and firmware is current; HSM malfunction → verify drivers and
  firmware version; encryption failures → confirm BIOS/OS/encryption software compatibility; slow performance → balance encryption
  strength against system capability, especially on older hardware.
  
  Best practices: regularly audit encryption configuration, pair encryption with multi-factor authentication (MFA), and keep all
  firmware/security patches current.

7. CPU Architecture
  
  x86/x64 (CISC — Complex Instruction Set Computing): used in most desktops/laptops; rich instruction set handles complex operations
  in fewer cycles — good for general-purpose computing. x86 (32-bit) is capped at 4GB RAM and is now legacy; x64 (64-bit) is the modern
  standard, supporting much more RAM, better memory handling, and hardware-based security features.
  
  ARM (RISC — Reduced Instruction Set Computing): optimized for power efficiency and simple instruction execution; dominant in
  smartphones, tablets, IoT, and increasingly laptops/cloud servers. Offers long battery life, high scalability, and built-in security
  (e.g., ARM TrustZone) — but has less legacy desktop software compatibility than x86/x64.
  
  Hybrid architectures: emerging designs that blend ARM's efficiency with x86/x64 performance, aiming to balance power consumption
  and processing power across laptops, tablets, and servers.
  
  Core configurations:
    
    - Single-core: executes one task at a time — largely obsolete for modern workloads.
    - Dual-core: balanced performance for standard tasks (browsing, word processing, light multitasking).
    - Quad-core: better suited to gaming and content creation.
    - Octa-core and higher: used in workstations/servers for heavy parallel workloads (3D rendering, simulations, virtual machines).
    
  Hyper-Threading (Intel) / SMT (AMD): lets each physical core handle multiple threads simultaneously, improving throughput for
  multi-threaded workloads like gaming, editing, and virtualization.
  
  Choosing an architecture: match to workload type (general use vs. high-performance computing), power needs (ARM for
  battery-powered/portable devices, x86/x64 for raw performance), and required software compatibility.

8. CPU Compatibility (Practical Selection)
  
  Three alignment points, same as motherboard-CPU compatibility above: socket type, chipset support, and BIOS/UEFI support.
  
  Intel sockets: LGA 1200 (10th/11th gen, mainstream), LGA 1700 (12th/13th gen, DDR5/PCIe 5.0 support), LGA 2066 (Core X-series, HEDT).
  AMD sockets: AM4 (Ryzen 1000–5000), AM5 (next-gen, DDR5), TR4/sTRX4 (Threadripper, workstation/HEDT).
  
  What chipsets control: PCIe lane availability, RAM speed/type compatibility, storage options, overclocking potential, and connectivity
  (USB count, Wi-Fi, RAID support).
  
  Chipset examples: Intel Z690 (high-end, PCIe 5.0/DDR5/overclocking), B660 (budget, fewer features), H770 (balanced); AMD B550
  (mainstream, PCIe 4.0), X570 (premium, full PCIe 4.0/overclocking), B650 (budget Ryzen 7000, DDR5/PCIe 5.0).
  
  Selecting a CPU: assess real performance needs (basic vs. gaming/rendering/3D work), plan an upgrade path, balance budget against
  performance, and always confirm compatibility using manufacturer tools before purchasing. Plan for adequate cooling and power supply
  capacity, especially for high-performance CPUs, and update motherboard firmware before installing a new processor.

9. Expansion Cards
  
  Add-on components installed in PCIe (or legacy PCI) slots to extend system capability:
    
    - GPUs (graphics cards): dedicated graphics rendering — essential for gaming, video editing, and graphically intensive work.
    - Sound cards: higher-fidelity audio processing than onboard sound — popular with audiophiles and audio production.
    - Network interface cards (NICs): add or upgrade wired/wireless connectivity.
    - Capture cards: record/stream video from external sources (consoles, cameras) — common in content creation and live streaming.
    
  Choosing a card: verify PCIe slot type/size (x1, x4, x8, x16), check power connector requirements (high-end GPUs often need dedicated
  power connectors), plan for adequate cooling, confirm driver/OS support, and match specs to the intended use case.
  
  Installation steps: power off and unplug the system → locate an appropriate PCIe slot → remove the case's I/O shield if needed → seat
  the card firmly and evenly → secure it with a screw → connect any required power cables → power on and install drivers.
  
  Best practices: use anti-static precautions (wrist strap) when handling cards, ensure sufficient case airflow/cooling (especially for
  GPUs), and keep drivers/firmware updated.
  
  Troubleshooting: card not detected → check seating, update drivers, verify BIOS settings; performance issues → confirm adequate power
  and cooling, update firmware; compatibility errors → confirm OS support and update motherboard firmware, check for driver conflicts;
  intermittent connectivity → reseat the card or try a different slot.
  
  Long-term maintenance: keep slots/contacts free of dust, monitor temperatures and maintain airflow, and regularly update drivers and
  firmware.

Quick Exam-Focus Reminders
  
  - Form factor size order, largest to smallest: EATX > ATX > MicroATX > Mini-ITX > Mini-STX > Nano-ITX > Pico-ITX.
  - CPU compatibility always comes down to three checks: socket type, chipset support, and BIOS/UEFI version.
  - BIOS = text-based, legacy, 2TB drive limit; UEFI = graphical, faster boot, >2TB drives, Secure Boot support.
  - TPM = on-motherboard key storage/crypto chip (used for BitLocker, Secure Boot, attestation); HSM = dedicated, physically isolated,
    tamper-resistant crypto device for high-security/enterprise environments.
  - x86/x64 = CISC, general-purpose performance, higher power draw; ARM = RISC, power-efficient, mobile/embedded-first, growing in
    laptops/servers.
  - Hyper-Threading (Intel) and SMT (AMD) both let one physical core run multiple threads — same concept, different vendor name.
  - Expansion card slot sizing: PCIe x1 (smallest/slowest) up through x16 (largest/fastest, typically used for GPUs).
  - Always update BIOS/firmware BEFORE installing a new CPU or a card that needs newer chipset support — mismatches cause boot failures.
